1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,273
Angstrom Signals comes with a library of Groups and Sets,

2
00:00:03,293 --> 00:00:05,847
built around common trading situations.

3
00:00:05,897 --> 00:00:09,141
In Prism, open Provided by Angstrom Signals.

4
00:00:09,609 --> 00:00:12,512
Each one shows small icons for what it looks at.

5
00:00:12,562 --> 00:00:16,339
Here: volume, and structure, the support and resistance levels.

6
00:00:16,781 --> 00:00:20,388
Underneath, one line says what it describes, in plain words.

7
00:00:20,829 --> 00:00:22,708
Tap more for three parts:

8
00:00:22,758 --> 00:00:24,210
what it measures,

9
00:00:24,260 --> 00:00:26,053
why traders watch it,

10
00:00:26,103 --> 00:00:27,875
and what it doesn't prove.

11
00:00:28,331 --> 00:00:30,914
The last icon is the evidence mark.

12
00:00:30,964 --> 00:00:33,397
A microscope means not yet studied.

13
00:00:33,447 --> 00:00:37,032
A balance means it was studied, and showed no clear difference.

14
00:00:37,082 --> 00:00:39,430
The next two videos explain both.

15
00:00:39,874 --> 00:00:43,182
To find one quickly, tap an icon in the filter row.

16
00:00:43,232 --> 00:00:45,985
Here, only the Sets that look at volatility.

17
00:00:46,424 --> 00:00:51,097
Tap plus, and a provided Set becomes one of your Prism scans, ready to run.

18
00:00:51,559 --> 00:00:55,294
Signal Groups and Signal Sets have the same library at the top.

19
00:00:55,344 --> 00:00:59,462
And the Start from list offers them too, after New, and before your own.

20
00:01:01,047 --> 00:01:04,526
Once you know them, one setting hides everything provided.

21
00:01:04,576 --> 00:01:06,945
Descriptive readings, not advice.
